HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) happens when the same query parameter appears more than once, e.g.:

```http
GET /search?q=safe&q=<script>alert(1)</script> HTTP/1.1
```

A WAF or validator may inspect only the first value (`safe`), while the backend framework may use the second (`<script>...`), or vice versa.

Example vulnerable handling:

```python
# Flask
from flask import request

q = request.args.get("q")  # often returns only the first value
# WAF may see "safe", app may later use a different/merged value
```

Safer handling:

```python
from flask import request, abort

values = request.args.getlist("q")
if len(values) != 1:
    abort(400, "Duplicate or missing parameter")

q = values[0]
if not q.isalnum():
    abort(400, "Invalid input")
```

Node/Express example:

```js
const qs = require("qs");

const parsed = qs.parse(req.url.split("?")[1] || "", {
  allowDots: false,
  strict: true
});

const q = parsed.q;

if (Array.isArray(q) || typeof q !== "string" || q.length === 0) {
  return res.status(400).send("Invalid parameter");
}

if (!/^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+$/.test(q)) {
  return res.status(400).send("Invalid input");
}
```

Test with:

```bash
curl "https://example.com/search?q=safe&q=<script>alert(1)</script>"
```

Key rule: reject duplicate parameters or explicitly define how they are merged, and make sure the WAF and application parse the query string the same way.
